WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.134 --> 00:00:02.810
(singers vocalizing)

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.793
- [Lisa] We are mixed race.

00:00:04.793 --> 00:00:06.350
♪ Oh, hey ♪

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.496
- [Lisa] We are black and white.

00:00:07.496 --> 00:00:08.461
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:00:08.461 --> 00:00:10.000
♪ Hey ♪

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.070
- [Lisa] We are French and Cuban,

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.350
and we have this African culture.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.932
- When your mom is on tour
with you, that's what you do.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:19.220
(Naomi chuckles)

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.120
- [Lisa] We really feel like
all of that is what makes us.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.653
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:31.502
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:31.502 --> 00:00:35.126
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:35.126 --> 00:00:36.886
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:36.886 --> 00:00:41.886
♪ Carried this ♪

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:45.192
♪ For years ♪

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:48.737
("I Carried This For Years" by Ibeyi)

00:00:48.737 --> 00:00:50.447
♪ I carried ♪

00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:52.628
♪ I carried ♪

00:00:52.628 --> 00:00:54.339
♪ I've carried ♪

00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:59.339
♪ I've carried ♪

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:01.771
♪ I've carried ♪

00:01:01.771 --> 00:01:03.982
♪ I've carried ♪

00:01:03.982 --> 00:01:06.565
♪ I've carried ♪

00:01:08.121 --> 00:01:11.540
(audience cheers)

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.330
- My name is Naomi Diaz.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.680
- I am Lisa-Kiandé; and we are ...

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:16.906
- [Unison] Ibeyi.

00:01:16.906 --> 00:01:19.323
♪ Away, Away ♪

00:01:20.928 --> 00:01:24.597
- [Lisa] Ibeyi means twins
in Yoruba, and we're twins.

00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:25.830
♪ Away, Away ♪

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:30.080
- [Lisa] I do the piano parts
and the melody, the lyrics.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.053
- [Naomi] I do all the
production and rhythm.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.960
- Basically, I do the skeleton,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.228
and she gives it the flesh and the skin.

00:01:40.228 --> 00:01:42.645
♪ Away, Away ♪

00:01:46.796 --> 00:01:49.629
(audience cheers)

00:01:53.911 --> 00:01:57.661
(motorcycle engines revving)

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.540
- We grew up listening to French artists

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:06.306
and old soul singers, Yoruba chants.

00:02:06.306 --> 00:02:10.139
(singing in foreign language)

00:02:19.078 --> 00:02:21.012
(singing in foreign language)

00:02:21.012 --> 00:02:22.710
- [Lisa] My mother took us to Yoruba Kwara

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.680
for the first time; we were 15.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.560
And we fell in love with those songs.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.830
And we are on stage every
night singing those songs.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:36.000
- [Naomi] We're honored to
be part of this culture.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.520
- It's almost like we are
sponges, so we take everything in;

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.100
and it mixes up inside our bellies,

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.850
and then it comes back and it sounds like.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:46.683
- [Unison] Ibeyi.

00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:47.809
(Naomi laughs)
- It's really weird,

00:02:47.809 --> 00:02:50.304
(Naomi laughs)
but it really is like that.

00:02:50.304 --> 00:02:53.887
♪ Should we just let it be ♪

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:59.090
- This is our song "Deathless"
from our second album, Ash.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:04.063
Normally, I come to the studio
with the piano and the melody

00:03:04.063 --> 00:03:05.650
and the lyrics.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.807
But this time, it started with
the beat and with the bass.

00:03:08.807 --> 00:03:13.807
(baseline plays)
It's heavy and dark.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.130
Now, me and Richard Russell, our producer,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.636
both created this up tempo beat.

00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:25.803
(drum beats and baseline playing)

00:03:27.687 --> 00:03:30.960
"Deathless" is a really
important song in our album.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.850
We wanted people to understand
the power they have.

00:03:35.902 --> 00:03:39.735
(singing in foreign language)

00:03:43.216 --> 00:03:47.883
When I was 16, I got stopped
on the subway by policeman.

00:03:49.602 --> 00:03:50.815
And there were like, "Do you smoke?"

00:03:50.815 --> 00:03:51.728
And I said, "No."

00:03:51.728 --> 00:03:53.868
And they were like, "Do you use drugs?"

00:03:53.868 --> 00:03:54.752
And I said, "No."

00:03:54.752 --> 00:03:55.631
"Do you drink?"

00:03:55.631 --> 00:03:56.502
And I said, "No."

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.800
And then they were like,
"Are you effing kidding me?"

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.830
and really, really close to my face.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.204
I didn't understand.

00:04:03.204 --> 00:04:05.663
Obviously, he was a racist.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.840
And then, he made me take
out my socks and my shoes.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.020
He took my bag and threw
everything on the floor like that.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.700
But, when he saw that I had
a huge book I was reading,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.110
Anna Karenina, he froze,
gave me my empty bag back,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.690
and left.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.683
And then, I forgot about it.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.107
But when our producer,
Richard Russell asked us,

00:04:32.107 --> 00:04:33.890
"Do you wanna write a song about it?"

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:38.300
I said, "I can't, my
story is like, nothing."

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.900
And Naomi said something incredible.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.410
- [Naomi] I said that it's
wrong, and it wasn't real enough,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.423
and that she should talk about it.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.110
- So after having this beat in my ears,

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.517
I started writing the
lyrics on exactly what

00:04:53.517 --> 00:04:56.035
this policeman said to me.

00:04:56.035 --> 00:04:59.110
♪ Do you smoke ♪

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.627
♪ What's your name ♪

00:05:02.627 --> 00:05:06.210
♪ Do you know why I'm here ♪

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:08.450
We realize that, actually,

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.077
what we wanted to do was an anthem

00:05:10.077 --> 00:05:12.640
for everybody to feel deathless,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.410
to feel immortal for three minutes,

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.310
for everybody to feel like
one, to feel their own power.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.654
And we created that hook.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:25.761
♪ Whatever happens, whatever happened ♪

00:05:25.761 --> 00:05:28.327
♪ Why ♪

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:31.457
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:05:31.457 --> 00:05:34.510
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:38.240
- So, usually, what we do
is we loop our own voices

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.100
over and over and over again.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.670
Because it's an anthem,
we need other voices,

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.493
so we called this amazing
choir, and we all sang.

00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:51.167
♪ Whatever happens, whatever happened ♪

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:53.351
♪ Oh ♪

00:05:53.351 --> 00:05:55.043
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:05:55.043 --> 00:05:56.831
- Even the sound guy.

00:05:56.831 --> 00:05:57.933
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:05:57.933 --> 00:05:59.610
- Even Richard Russell, our producer.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:00.670
Our mother was there.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.610
We all sang "We Are Deathless!" Together,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.980
and it was an incredible experience.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.520
So, once we had the beat,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.660
and actually the melody and the lyrics,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:14.623
we went back to the studio
and you added your drums.

00:06:17.416 --> 00:06:19.599
(chuckles) Love your face.

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:22.820
(snickers) You look so
depressed in that photo.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:23.710
- That's our dad.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.728
- You were like, oh, I'm done.

00:06:25.728 --> 00:06:28.811
(drum beats playing)

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.460
We grew up with our father being

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.720
one of the stars of the Latin jazz world,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.841
a master of the percussion.

00:06:37.841 --> 00:06:41.762
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:41.762 --> 00:06:42.688
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:42.688 --> 00:06:43.521
(woman laughs)

00:06:43.521 --> 00:06:44.990
(Naomi laughs)

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.826
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:47.826 --> 00:06:51.409
(drum beats playing)

00:06:51.409 --> 00:06:53.920
(drum beats playing)
(Miguel chanting)

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.130
- When our father died, we were 11.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:57.410
That same year, we were like,

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.835
OK, let's try to make a band.

00:06:59.835 --> 00:07:01.910
(drum beats playing)

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.825
- [Lisa] When she played the
cajón for the first time,

00:07:03.825 --> 00:07:06.707
there's something that
happened, and we all felt it.

00:07:06.707 --> 00:07:09.020
It was like, this is it.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.773
It was not just the rhythm; it was lived.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:15.495
Our father's genes were in her.

00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:18.883
It was almost like you had
played for years already.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.063
- I play the cajón or the beta cajón.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.200
It's what we use for Yoruba
traditional ceremony in Cuba.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:32.350
Normally, it is made out of skin.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:36.310
My master, Miguel Ballumbrosio,
he does it with wood.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.420
So, he made this for me.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:43.420
We really like to mix
electronic sounds and some wood.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.075
And so, I just find that rhythm.

00:07:47.075 --> 00:07:48.450
(drum beats playing)

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.980
- It could be one day, a hip-hop beat

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:54.210
that she actually deconstructs,

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.400
or it could be a pop beat,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.650
but it could also can
be a traditional pattern

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.764
from the Yoruba culture.

00:08:01.764 --> 00:08:05.231
(drum beats playing)
We found a base sound

00:08:05.231 --> 00:08:10.231
when we combined voices,
traditional drums, and wood,

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.310
and electronics.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:15.153
And this is when it all clicks.

00:08:15.153 --> 00:08:19.734
♪ Left for dead in the streets ♪

00:08:19.734 --> 00:08:23.666
♪ Whatever happens, whatever happened ♪

00:08:23.666 --> 00:08:26.173
♪ Oh ♪

00:08:26.173 --> 00:08:27.940
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.990
- There's some songs that
are really hard to do,

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:31.180
and there's some songs that

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.550
you don't know why it's almost magical.

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:37.412
Everything fits, and
that's what that song is.

00:08:37.412 --> 00:08:39.114
♪ Oh ♪

00:08:39.114 --> 00:08:40.930
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.448
- Our whole love for music
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:08:43.448 --> 00:08:44.373
is mixing.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.420
We are proud to mix everything,

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.109
and for it to still feel that
it comes from your heart.

00:08:51.109 --> 00:08:52.733
♪ Oh ♪

00:08:52.733 --> 00:08:54.255
♪ We are deathless ♪
- Music has the power that

00:08:54.255 --> 00:08:55.942
has no boundaries.

00:08:55.942 --> 00:08:59.422
♪ Whatever happens, whatever happened ♪

00:08:59.422 --> 00:09:01.794
♪ Oh ♪

00:09:01.794 --> 00:09:05.049
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:09:05.049 --> 00:09:07.966
♪ We are deathless ♪

00:09:10.805 --> 00:09:13.722
(music note plays)

